  "title": "Chapter 48: Budget and Taxation in India",
  "order": 48,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "on3c37n",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Budget"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Constitution of India refers to the budget as the Annual Financial Statement under Article 112.",
        "The Financial Year in India runs from April 1 of the current year to March 31 of the following year.",
        "Government Receipts are broadly categorized into Revenue Receipts (non-liability creating) and Capital Receipts (liability creating or asset reducing).",
        "Direct Taxes are paid directly by individuals/corporations, whereas Indirect Taxes like GST are levied on goods and services.",
        "Goods and Services Tax (GST) was introduced via the 101st Constitutional Amendment Act and came into effect on July 1, 2017.",
        "Fiscal Deficit indicates the total borrowing requirements of the government during a financial year."
      ],
      "id": "dspp8mz"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Constitutional Article",
          "value": "Article 112",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Financial Year Period",
          "value": "1 April - 31 March",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "GST Launch Year",
          "value": "2017",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Main Components",
          "value": "Revenue & Capital",
          "icon": "info"
        }
      ],
      "id": "jymgu6i"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "61xh4gf",
      "content": "The budget is the annual financial statement of the government showing estimated revenue and expenditure for a financial year. In India, the Union Budget is presented by the Finance Minister in Parliament. According to Article 112 of the Constitution, the budget is known as the Annual Financial Statement."
    },
    {
      "id": "pcxcad3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Financial Year in India"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mfdhd1l",
      "content": "The financial year in India runs from 1 April to 31 March of the next year."
    },
    {
      "id": "vbvxuuj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Objectives of Government Budget"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bczcfmf",
      "content": "• Economic growth and development"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bk4hu0a",
      "content": "• Reduction of poverty"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vfdsqxk",
      "content": "• Employment generation"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "1a21fbp",
      "content": "• Price stability"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ms3pnta",
      "content": "• Reduction of income inequality"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uwh283f",
      "content": "• Infrastructure development"
    },
    {
      "id": "lc3qcex",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Components of Government Budget"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3jwvzm",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Component</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Revenue Budget</p>",
            "<p>Includes revenue receipts and revenue expenditure</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Capital Budget</p>",
            "<p>Includes capital receipts and capital expenditure</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "uj8dizm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Revenue Receipts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "raeivo2",
      "content": "Revenue receipts are income received by the government without creating liabilities."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4t6r74i",
      "content": "Types:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jp0y36l",
      "content": "• Tax Revenue – Income tax, GST, corporate tax"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "akkk9qt",
      "content": "• Non-Tax Revenue – Fees, fines, dividends"
    },
    {
      "id": "d75xvsr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Capital Receipts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "opu7g4a",
      "content": "Capital receipts create liabilities or reduce government assets."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a62yght",
      "content": "Examples:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "o07l3no",
      "content": "• Borrowings"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "wbcxpye",
      "content": "• Disinvestment"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "a4ukwzx",
      "content": "• Recovery of loans"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Revenue Receipts",
        "Capital Receipts"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Liability Impact",
          "Neither creates liability nor reduces assets",
          "Creates liability or reduces assets"
        ],
        [
          "Nature",
          "Regular and recurring",
          "Irregular and non-recurring"
        ],
        [
          "Examples",
          "Tax revenue, fees, dividends, interest received",
          "Borrowings, disinvestment, loan recovery"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "iz6h08f"
    },
    {
      "id": "9tbq1pi",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Government Expenditure"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8nt6mbt",
      "content": "Government expenditure is divided into revenue expenditure and capital expenditure."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fmrff8p",
      "content": "Revenue Expenditure Examples: Salaries, subsidies, interest payments."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zijz8i3",
      "content": "Capital Expenditure Examples: Infrastructure projects, roads, dams."
    },
    {
      "id": "ny950y1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Types of Taxes"
    },
    {
      "id": "i0lemxi",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Tax Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Examples</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Direct Taxes</p>",
            "<p>Income tax, corporate tax</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Indirect Taxes</p>",
            "<p>GST, customs duty, excise duty</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "r81iqmt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "9. Goods and Services Tax (GST)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hg1zhse",
      "content": "• Introduced on 1 July 2017"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ylnl2gu",
      "content": "• Replaced many indirect taxes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "do7v9un",
      "content": "• Known as 'One Nation One Tax' reform"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8zrkl85",
      "content": "• Types: CGST, SGST and IGST"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Article 112 mandates the Annual Financial Statement; Article 110 defines Money Bills.",
        "Fiscal Deficit equals Total Expenditure minus Total Receipts excluding borrowings.",
        "GST is a destination-based indirect tax introduced by the 101st Amendment Act, 2016.",
        "Primary Deficit is calculated as Fiscal Deficit minus Interest Payments."
      ],
      "id": "rvjc5rm"
    },
    {
      "id": "2dk3xa4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10. Budget Deficit"
    },
    {
      "id": "4m4quo3",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Deficit Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Meaning</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Fiscal Deficit</p>",
            "<p>Total expenditure minus total revenue</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Revenue Deficit</p>",
            "<p>Revenue expenditure minus revenue receipts</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Primary Deficit</p>",
            "<p>Fiscal deficit minus interest payments</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "yih5bez",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ngo0rih",
      "content": "• Union Budget is presented by the Finance Minister."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f1atvny",
      "content": "• Financial year in India is April to March."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l5aio86",
      "content": "• GST was implemented in India in 2017."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d08fk1v",
      "content": "• Article 112 of the Constitution deals with the Union Budget."
    }
  ],
